/-!
# The Hilbert–Serre theorem

Let `M = ⨁_{d : ℤ} ℳ d` be a finitely generated `ℤ`-graded module over the standard polynomial ring
`MvPolynomial σ A` on `e = Fintype.card σ` variables (where the grading allows generators of
negative degree). General finiteness and lower-degree vanishing of graded pieces are developed in
`TauCeti.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.FiniteType` and `TauCeti.Algebra.Module.GradedModule.Finite`.

Over a commutative Artinian ring `A`, every graded piece `ℳ d` is a finite `A`-module of finite
length (`TauCeti.GradedModule.moduleFinite_piece_mvPolynomial`,
`TauCeti.GradedModule.length_piece_ne_top`) and vanishes for all `d` below some integer `d₀`
(`TauCeti.GradedModule.exists_forall_lt_piece_eq_bot`). Inducting on `e` via multiplication by the
last variable `X (Fin.last e)` — whose kernel and cokernel are finitely generated `ℤ`-graded modules
over `e - 1` variables, by the Hilbert basis theorem since `A` is Artinian hence Noetherian — yields
a polynomial `h : ℤ[X]` such that
`(∑ n, (Module.length A (ℳ (d₀ + n))).toNat • Xⁿ) * (1 - X)^e = h` in `ℤ⟦X⟧`. More generally, for
any commutative ring `A` and any starting degree `d₀ : ℤ`, the formal series of `ENat.toNat` length
coefficients `(Module.length A (ℳ (d₀ + n))).toNat` — where `ENat.toNat ⊤ = 0`, so infinite-length
pieces contribute `0` — still satisfies this rational identity
(`exists_hilbertSeries_mul_one_sub_X_pow`), because any single finite-length piece in sufficiently
high degree generates the tail of `M` and has Artinian annihilator quotient
(`TauCeti.isArtinianRing_quotient_annihilator`); this series agrees with the genuine length Hilbert
series whenever every piece has finite length (for instance when `A` is Artinian).

Translating Mathlib's `Polynomial.hilbertPoly` by `d₀` then supplies the eventual Hilbert
polynomial `P : ℚ[X]` of degree `< e` agreeing with `Module.length A (ℳ d)` for all sufficiently
large `d : ℤ` (`hilbertSerre`). By `exists_int_eval_of_eventually_eval_length`, any eventual
polynomial for the `ENat.toNat` length coefficients over a ring with unique additive division has
degree `< e` and takes integer values at every integer `z`. Restricting scalars along evaluation at
finitely many algebra generators that shift grading by `1` transfers the theorem to any standard
finitely generated `A`-algebra (`hilbertSerre_of_adjoin_eq_top`), and over a field `k` lengths
coincide with `Module.finrank` and the theorem also records integer values at all integers
(`hilbertSerre_field`). Finally, homogeneous short exact sequences add lengths piece by piece
(`length_piece_eq_add_of_exact`) and hence add eventual Hilbert polynomials
(`hilbertPoly_add_of_exact`), while shifting degrees by `a` substitutes `X + C a`
(`hilbertPoly_shift`).

## Main results

* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.length_piece_eq_add_of_exact`: additivity of piece lengths in a
  homogeneous short exact sequence over any cancellative grading monoid.
* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.length_piece_add_quotient_eq_add_ker`: alternating-length identity for a
  homogeneous endomorphism.
* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.exists_hilbertSeries_mul_one_sub_X_pow`: rational form `h / (1 - X)^e` in
  `ℤ⟦X⟧` of the shifted series of `ENat.toNat` length coefficients over any commutative ring and
  from any starting degree (with `ENat.toNat ⊤ = 0`; equals the genuine length Hilbert series when
  every piece has finite length, e.g. when `A` is Artinian).
* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.hilbertSerre`: the Hilbert–Serre theorem for finitely generated `ℤ`-graded
  modules over `MvPolynomial σ A` for an Artinian ring `A`.
* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.exists_int_eval_of_eventually_eval_length`: degree bound and integer values
  at all integers for any eventual polynomial of the `ENat.toNat` length coefficients.
* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.hilbertSerre_of_adjoin_eq_top`: the Hilbert–Serre theorem for algebras
  generated by finitely many elements that shift the module grading by `1`.
* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.hilbertSerre_field`: the dimension-polynomial specialization over a field.
* `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.hilbertPoly_add_of_exact` and `TauCeti.GradedAlgebra.hilbertPoly_shift`:
  additivity in degree-zero short exact sequences and behavior under degree shifts.

## References

* M. F. Atiyah and I. G. Macdonald, *Introduction to Commutative Algebra*, Addison–Wesley (1969),
  Chapter 11, Theorem 11.1.
* The Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemmas 10.58.5–10.58.7 (tags 00K7, 00K8, 00K9),
  <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00K9>.
-/

